<p>Today's brief covers stories from 1 to 3 November that earlier briefs did not include.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Detect</b><span>Telemetry shows an agent behaving outside its expected pattern</span></li>
<li><b>Decide</b><span>A person reviews the evidence and chooses to intervene</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Cut model access</b><span>The AI gateway stops the agent reaching its models</span></li>
<li><b>Contain</b><span>The agent is stopped on its hosts and blocked from restarting</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Isolate</b><span>Its network access is cut while the incident is investigated</span></li>
<li><b>Restore</b><span>Access returns only after a person approves</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The shape of an agent kill switch: the gateway is the control point, and people make the calls.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. CrowdStrike launches a kill switch for rogue AI agents</h2>
<p>CrowdStrike announced an AI Kill Switch on 3 November that lets administrators stop a misbehaving agent, block it from restarting on targeted hosts and cut its network access; the first version works through connected AI gateways by cutting the agent's access to its models, with broader containment across its platform still to come<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. CNBC reports a person still decides when to cut an agent off and when to restore it, and that CrowdStrike also agreed to buy two AI security start-ups<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> this confirms where agent control belongs: at a gateway between agents and models, with identity per agent and a clear way to stop one. Whether or not you buy a product for it, you need those three things before agents run in production. See <a href="/insights/llmjacking-stolen-ai-keys-black-october/">why an AI gateway matters</a> and <a href="/insights/agents-replace-seats-enterprise-saas/">when agents become the user</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. AMD's data centre revenue nearly doubles</h2>
<p>AMD reported record third-quarter revenue of about $13.4 billion on 3 November, with data centre revenue up 97% year over year to a record $8.5 billion, and guided to about $17 billion for the fourth quarter<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> a credible second source of AI accelerators is good news for buyers' negotiating position and capacity planning. Keep your serving stack portable across hardware, for example with vLLM and Kubernetes device abstractions, so you can benchmark and switch; see <a href="/insights/ai-memory-squeeze-inference-architecture/">the AI memory squeeze</a> and <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">DRA for GPU scheduling</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Attackers drain hot wallets on outdated BTCPay Servers</h2>
<p>Attackers have been draining Bitcoin and Litecoin hot wallets on BTCPay Server installations running version 2.4.1 or earlier; a fix shipped in 2.4.2, and BTCPay told operators running hot wallets to shut down affected servers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The exact vulnerability behind the latest thefts had not been published at the time of writing<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> self-hosted payment software is only as safe as its patch level. If you run it, update, keep only operational funds in hot wallets, rotate credentials and move funds to new wallets if in doubt. The same money-path principles apply as in <a href="/insights/binance-hack-custody-architecture-lessons/">the Binance lessons</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Android's November update fixes zero-click remote code execution</h2>
<p>Google's November 2026 Android security bulletin includes critical remote code execution flaws that need no user interaction, affecting Android 14 to 17, along with a denial-of-service bug dubbed "SMS of Death" that can drop calls; reports say the bulletin does not list any issue as exploited<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> make sure company-managed devices pick up the November patch level quickly, and set a minimum patch level for apps that handle money or health data. For bring-your-own-device fleets, enforce the minimum through your device management policy.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Check that every AI agent in production has its own identity, goes through a gateway and can be stopped.</li>
<li>Update BTCPay Server if you run it, and reduce hot wallet balances.</li>
<li>Push the November Android patch level to managed devices.</li>
<li>Ask accelerator vendors for quotes on more than one platform before your next hardware order.</li>
</ul>
<p>See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
